r.append(record('2026-2481',[
    interview(u,'Team 2481 Roboteers interview','0:43–1:38 launcher; 2:23–4:16 collector; 4:34–6:34 storage and floor; 6:48–9:13 driving and practice',
              ['Four-wide shooter uses staged acceleration.','The team added a collector motor after stalls and changed wall supports after catches and racking.','Bump-only travel and loaded gearing were part of the scoring strategy.'],
              'Reported 20 BPS and 115 fuel are team claims. The interview does not establish independent capacity or full-match throughput.')
], 'Roboteers built a large fuel carrier whose value depended on unloading a useful batch through a wide shooter. The robot traded the trench route for more volume and took the bump with that load. Its collector, hopper walls, feeder and wheel gearing therefore belong in the same design decision. An empty sprint or a bench burst would leave the difficult parts of the architecture untested.',
['The wide roller gathers fuel while a relief plate reduces direct bulk pressure on the collector.','Fuel fills the deployable volume and settles onto interlocking floor rollers.','The floor and serial contact stages move fuel into four launch lanes. The top contact conditions fuel entry without requiring another driven roller in that position.'],
'The team describes a relatively narrow chassis dimension that makes the direction of bump crossing matter. The larger loaded footprint comes from extending walls. Heavy hardware sits low, but fuel raises the combined center of mass and can surge as the robot starts up the bump. The route must be wide enough to cross on a repeatable heading even when a defender approaches.',
[
    m('Collector and pressure relief','Three-inch grip-covered intake roller with two motors in the reported later setup. A plate limits fuel weight pressing directly on the intake.','Keep the intake running while fuel accumulates just inside the mouth.','The team added the second motor after stall problems.','Separate lack of available torque from an unfavorable contact load. A pressure barrier can prevent bulk inventory from continuously loading a roller intended to accelerate one layer at a time.','The roller opening must stay useful across a wide mouth while walls retract and extend.','A pressure-relief feature can become a pinch point if fuel wedges under it. Test new and worn balls while pushing against a full bin.','Precise gaps, currents and endurance data are unresolved.'),
    m('Expanding hopper and floor','Flexible side structure and a roller floor feed the four-wide launch section. The team revised wall support geometry.','Deploy the hopper for bulk collection. The floor presents lower fuel while the rest of the load settles.','The team changed supports after racking and catches.','More static volume gives diminishing returns if the added fuel bridges above the floor, spills during travel or takes longer than the active scoring window to clear.','The floor must coexist with electronics and route-clearance requirements. The stowed wall envelope sets bump clearance and access to the drivetrain.','Measure load retention over repeated crossings and examine where a bent wall first intrudes into the roller path.','The 115-fuel estimate is a source claim under unspecified packing conditions.'),
    m('Four-wide launcher','Multiple accelerator contacts feed a wide launch assembly. A passive upper contact and electric hood actuation are described.','Build fuel speed over successive contacts, then launch at the selected angle and flywheel speed.','The extra upper contact improved the entrance without adding a powered roller in that location.','If launch contact force varies by lane, equal speed commands do not imply equal release speed or trajectory. Measure misses by lane and shooter speed recovery during a full burst.','The floor and shooter need equal flow across the width. A single loaded lane can reduce consistency even if the motor has ample total power.','Bracket repairs and actuator changes show the value of replacing a high-load mounting point without rebuilding the floor.','Reported BPS lacks a confirmed measurement window and scoring conversion rate.'),
    m('Loaded drivetrain and routes','The team describes custom adjustable-gearing swerve, large wheels and a chassis shaped to cross the bump.','Approach in the planned direction, climb the obstacle loaded and return to a scoring position.','The team chose gearing for loaded current and the crossing rather than maximum possible speed.','Compare the obstacle route with the alternative under realistic traffic. More fuel per trip can still lose if repeated turns, beaching or queueing consume the scoring window.','Drive tuning, fuel retention and the approach geometry are coupled. Electrical demand rises when the drivetrain and feeder operate together.','Test from a partially discharged battery and a poor entry angle. The team credited practice as well as mechanical changes.','No controlled timing across routes, configurations and driver practice levels.')
], ['Collector changed from one motor to two after stalling.','Hopper support geometry revised after catches and racking.','Electric hood actuator replaced a pneumatic approach in the reported configuration.'],
['Test a loaded route before choosing gearing or capacity.','Keep intake roller force separate from hopper inventory where practical.','Compare scored fuel per complete cycle, including travel, stalled feeds and misses.'],
['The trench route can reverse the capacity trade if it removes enough defense exposure or travel time.','A smaller hopper can match effective output when the active window or the feeder sets the limit.'],
['No independent match-by-match cycle sample or controlled comparison of shooter configurations.']))

u='https://youtu.be/ksvuenS2gGY'
m=lambda *x:mechanism(*x,[u])
r.append(record('2026-1114',[
    interview(u,'Team 1114 Simbotics interview','0:35–1:19 collector; 1:58–2:27 retracting kicker; 3:06–4:24 floor and feeder; 4:34–6:08 route and controls',
              ['Collector and kicker feed an extending hopper.','Side agitators, floor rollers and a rising feeder form separate transitions.','A kicker originally stayed out and was linked to hopper retraction.'],
              'The interview reports roughly 80 fuel and a bump-oriented strategy. It does not provide measured inventory or full competition CAD.')
], 'Simbotics used a tall hopper and a drum shooter to carry and discharge a large batch. The route choice was tied to the physical system. The robot used the bump, which meant the collector, kicker and extension had to retract predictably before travel. The important engineering work is in the transitions between the intake, floor and rising feed. Each change either removed a dead spot or reduced the chance that the robot would be stuck in a geometry the driver could not use.',
['The collector and lower kicker deliver fuel into the expanding hopper.','Side agitators loosen packed fuel and guide it toward the powered floor. Passive rollers occupy some spaces where fuel had stalled.','A compliant feeder forces fuel into the rising roller section. That section supplies the drum shooter at the height and approach it needs.'],
'The hopper, kicker and collector share the front envelope. Retraction folds the kicker as the hopper returns. This mechanical dependency can simplify operator commands, but the deployed geometry must still remain robust under a partially filled or deformed bin. A tall loaded hopper changes visibility, impact loading and the response of the chassis on the bump.',
[
    m('Collector and folding kicker','The main roller and kicker send fuel up into the hopper. The kicker retracts with linked hopper motion.','Deploy for acquisition. Retract the hopper to fold the kicker before crossing.','The earlier kicker could drop and stay extended. The linked version addresses that route problem.','A mechanical dependency avoids requiring a separately timed command. The design still needs hard stops and enough freedom to retract with debris or misalignment.','The folded kicker must clear the frame and loaded hopper. The collector can also become a contact point during recovery from an obstacle.','Check partial stow after a hit and a stalled extension actuator. Look for a positive indication that the route envelope has been restored.','No exact linkage dimensions or clearance tolerance in the interview.'),
    m('Hopper and powered floor','Extending storage supplies a rolling floor with side agitators and passive contact elements.','Use agitation to move fuel out of side and rear dead spots and into the floor’s direction of travel.','The team added passive rollers and high-grip surfaces after finding collection dead spots.','The floor cannot pull fuel that is supported by its neighbors above the active contacts. Side agitation can destabilize a bridge while avoiding a steep drop or additional transfer.','Wall and floor positions must maintain a usable exit at all fill levels. A twisted belt drive couples motions and imposes belt routing constraints.','Use a transparent mockup to locate stationary fuel during sustained output. Check belt tracking after impact and compare full with nearly empty behavior.','No measured live inventory or jam rate.'),
    m('Up-feed and drum','A compliant-wheel feeder drives fuel into a belt-coupled rising roller path and drum launcher.','Capture the floor output, raise the stream and place fuel into the launch contact.','Added grip helped drive fuel down into the feed entrance.','The most consequential gap may be a transition before the shooter, even when the shooter has ample acceleration. The rise should control the piece rather than counting on the next roller to recover a bouncing feed.','The floor angle, feeder nip and rising path need matched speeds and overlapping control.','Watch for a stalled feed wheel under bulk pressure and whether reversing can clear the same path without backing the hopper up.','Exact shooter angle, reductions and current limits unresolved.'),
    m('Driving and operator controls','The team describes multi-camera localization and intake states that change agitation behavior.','Stage the robot for scoring, signal readiness and use the known bump route with the hopper stowed as needed.','The team selected the bump and a drum design for its expected traffic and preferred play.','More inventory shifts the choice toward fewer trips. Driving around defense and aligning from a useful heading can still dominate the cycle.','Localization, vehicle heading and feed-ready logic determine whether a full mechanism produces a timely scoring burst.','Test a bad localization update, blocked route and beach recovery with a loaded bin.','No independent telemetry for shot pose, ready-state accuracy or route times.')
], ['Kicker retraction was linked to the hopper.','Passive contacts and additional grip addressed fuel dead spots.'],
['Link motions when they always need to occur together and the combined mechanism still has a reliable fallback.','Treat floor-to-shooter transitions as distinct from theoretical launch capacity.'],
['Independent actuation is useful when one mechanism must stay deployed while the other retracts.','A low-volume trench design can win the route trade where a loaded bump approach repeatedly loses time.'],
['As-built layout, loaded crossing margins, distributions of sustained scoring rate and jam recovery.']))

u='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kzn_t-LsDB8'
