r.append(record('2026-125',[
    interview(u,'Team 125 NUTRONs interview','0:42–1:44 frame; 2:12–3:54 collector; 4:38–5:50 serializer; 5:52–7:34 turret; 9:22–12:59 controls and autonomous',
              ['Trapezoidal frame gives the dish space while leaving room for modules.','The team replaced a dual-roller intake with a continuous contact drum and revised the serializer center.','A rotating capture dish and central up-feed drive independently, with a low inset turret above.'],
              'Some reported values are configuration-specific. Captions do not authenticate event installation or hidden geometry.')
], 'NUTRONs shaped the entire machine around the circular serializer. The chassis gives the moving capture mechanism a large footprint. Fuel exits the dish through the center, allowing one vertical path to supply the rotating shooter. This architecture buys freedom in where and when the robot can aim. It spends chassis volume, integration time and moving-shot tuning to get it. That makes it a useful rival for a wide fixed shooter, especially when evaluating whether aiming freedom pays for the feed and packaging cost.',
['The removable four-bar collector uses a continuous roller contact to pull fuel across the bumper.','Fuel settles into the enlarged circular working dish. The rotating pickup brings an intake point to pieces around the perimeter.','The independent center uptake captures fuel and carries it to the turret. Additional contacts close the dead region near the axis.','The turret sets yaw, the launcher establishes speed and the hood sets exit elevation. The hood has a low travel pose.'],
'The frame widens where the collector and dish need area and narrows toward the back. The interview describes a mix of stock and altered module packaging. A coaxial handoff reduces dependence on turret azimuth, but the rotating feeder, center column, wires, shooter sweep and access panels still need independent space. Keep the ground-to-dish and dish-to-column transitions serviceable after the superstructure is installed.',
[
    m('Chassis around the dish','Wide front and tighter rear rails form a trapezoid around the circular fuel working volume. The rear modules are packaged around the dish.','Drive a low-profile route while leaving the center available for the serializer and launcher.','The team selected the chassis shape to accommodate the enlarged dish.','Draw the actual dish working diameter and wheel envelopes before comparing frame shapes. Chassis corners that appear unused can still carry bumper mounts, wiring, battery access or collision loads.','Frame rails must support the dish and leave enough room for module extraction.','Check whether a wheel or module can be replaced without disturbing dish alignment. Inspect rear mounts after side impacts.','Exact tolerances and installed mass distribution were not measured.'),
    m('Four-bar collector','Removable four-bar with a continuous roller and kicker geometry. The team describes a small set of shoulder bolts at the module interface.','Set the mouth low enough for fuel acquisition and carry fuel over the bumper into the dish.','Earlier separated contact geometry jammed. The continuous roller reduces gaps in coverage.','A broad mouth can collect more fuel per approach, but weak coverage near supports can make one edge the practical bottleneck. An accessible removal joint limits the cost of impact repairs.','The extended mouth and retracting bars must clear the bumper and keep the low travel profile.','Compare edge pickup and simultaneous arrivals, then inspect the mount after offset contacts.','No measured approach tolerance or durability trials.'),
    m('Rotating capture and up-feed','A rotating dish and a separately driven central up-feed share an axis. Additional wheels address a center dead zone.','Move the pickup point to fuel around the dish and lift it in a single stream to the turret.','The team kept the serializer for moving-shot flexibility and made the working dish larger.','Independent speeds let the mechanism tune capture availability separately from the upward feed. A jam at the central intake can still starve a ready launcher.','The center outlet must deliver consistent piece position across dish orientations and turret motion.','Measure recovery from two pieces entering together and the fraction of fuel stranded near the center.','Team-reported working dish counts are configuration estimates. Bulk hopper capacity is a different quantity.'),
    m('Turret, hood and moving shot','Inset yaw drive, multi-motor launcher and lowering hood. The team discusses interpolation and motion compensation.','Select range-based settings, move to target yaw and adjust the shot for the robot’s motion and flight time.','The team chose the architecture to score or feed while moving around defense.','Compare the fraction of usable field time when the robot can collect and shoot with an alternative that turns the chassis. The value of yaw freedom depends on maintained localization, shot readiness and a reliable single feed.','Hood lowering determines trench travel clearance. Cable limits constrain turret travel and where the scoring pose can resume.','Verify a missed camera update, a shot at a yaw limit and recovery after multiple fuel loads.','No independent accuracy map or matched comparison with a fixed shooter.')
], ['Frame and serializer enlarged together.','Dual collector arrangement changed to a continuous roller.','Central uptake contacts and launcher power were revised.'],
['Lay out the feeder, frame and drive modules together when a large round component sets the envelope.','Compare shooting flexibility against the number of pieces delivered and scored per whole cycle.','Use a constrained test to separate a poor center handoff from a slow launch mechanism.'],
['A fixed wide shooter can win where the scoring route naturally provides the required heading and full-bin throughput matters more.','A rotating shooter can earn its complexity when usable moving shots preserve collection routes and reduce defender control.'],
['Configuration-by-event mapping, complete mechanical dimensions, measured workload, accuracy and scoring-rate distributions.']))

